# The preview image's old, unversioned URL.
#
# Must stay ABOVE the catch-all. Without this rule `/social-preview.png` falls
# through to `/index.html` and answers 200 text/html — so a crawler cache that
# DOES revalidate would replace a stale-but-valid image with an HTML document
# and the preview would break outright, which is worse than the stale image we
# are fixing. 301 sends those caches to the current asset instead.
#
# Generated by scripts/build-social-preview.mjs; the hash is the image's own.
/social-preview.png  /social-preview.7a4dd3e0.png  301

# Short aliases for the two most-linked legal pages.
#
# 301 to the canonical route, NOT a 200 rewrite to index.html. A rewrite would
# boot the SPA at the path `/privacy`, which is matched by the `/*` route in
# App.tsx — and that route is wrapped in ProtectedRoute, so a signed-out
# visitor following a link to /privacy would be bounced to the login page. A
# privacy policy nobody can read without an account is not a published privacy
# policy.
#
# Redirecting also keeps ONE canonical URL per document, which is what a
# citation in a contract or an email should point at.
#
# Must stay ABOVE the catch-all. Netlify applies the first match, so a rule
# below `/*` never fires — the same trap as the social-preview rule above.
#
# Note this is about the PAGES. The document text is served separately as
# static markdown at /legal/<id>.md. A real file wins over the catch-all in
# Netlify, so that path resolves to the file once published and falls through
# to index.html when it has not been (which LegalDocumentPage detects by
# checking whether the response body is HTML).
#
# Replaces two earlier rules pointing at docs/legal/site/*.html. Those
# hand-written pages are retired: they were a second copy of legal text with no
# rule about which governed. docs/legal/README.md explains.
/privacy  /legal/privacy-policy  301
/terms    /legal/terms-of-use    301

/*  /index.html  200
